artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the setting of reinforcement learning (RL), in which an agent interacts with an environment modeled as a Markov decision process (S, A, P, R, γ) consisting of a state space S, action space A, transition kernel P(s'|s,a), reward function R(s,a), and discount factor γ ∈ [0,1), with the goal of learning a policy π: S → A that maximizes the expected discounted return E[Σ_t γ^t R(s_t, a_t)]. A "physics prior" is any source of structured prior knowledge about the environment that arises from physical laws — for example, ordinary or partial differential equations governing dynamics, conservation laws (energy, momentum, mass), symmetries and invariances, dimensional analysis constraints, Lagrangian/Hamiltonian structure, known equilibrium or stability conditions, or approximate analytical/simulated models. A physics-informed reinforcement learning (PIRL) algorithm is one that integrates one or more such physics priors into one or more components of the RL pipeline (state/action representation, environment/dynamics model, reward shaping, policy or value-function class, exploration strategy, loss regularization, or safety constraints).

Problem. Construct and rigorously specify a comprehensive selection-and-organization framework F that, given as input a description of a novel physical task T (including available physical knowledge about T and the RL components it admits), outputs a principled choice and arrangement of physics priors to be embedded into the RL algorithm for T. The framework F must satisfy:

(i) Generality: F applies across physical domains and is not tailored to a single task;
(ii) Variability handling: F accommodates the empirical fact that the most effective physics priors may differ between tasks even within the same physical domain;
(iii) Component placement: F prescribes at which stage(s) of the RL pipeline each selected prior is integrated;
(iv) Reusability: F reduces the need to engineer each PIRL task from scratch, providing a systematic procedure rather than ad hoc design;
(v) Justification: F comes with criteria (theoretical, statistical, or empirical) for why the prescribed choice of priors is appropriate for T.

Provide such a framework, together with a precise specification of its inputs, outputs, decision procedure, and the criteria used to validate its prescriptions on novel physical tasks.

